WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.458
[MUSIC - "MY COUNTRY TIS OF
 THEE"]

00:00:01.458 --> 00:00:07.154
(SINGING) My
country tis of thee.

00:00:07.154 --> 00:00:11.026
Sweet land of liberty.

00:00:20.962 --> 00:00:22.420
DOUG JEFFREY: Ladies
and gentlemen,

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.640
if I could have your
attention, again, please.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.740
First off, I'd like to welcome
back our online audience.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.530
As you've heard,
over 50,000 people

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.480
registered online
for tonight's event.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.350
And second, for those
of you here present,

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:50.930
please continue with your coffee
and desserts as we proceed.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:54.020
It's my duty now to
introduce, again, my boss--

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.090
the president of
Hillsdale College--

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.836
for some remarks.

00:00:57.836 --> 00:00:59.210
And I thought I'd
do that tonight

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.633
by giving you an idea of
how he manages the college.

00:01:05.351 --> 00:01:07.880
And in doing that,
also draw a contrast

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.190
between the governing
of Hillsdale and the way

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.150
government operates
here in this city.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.340
So I won't provide you
the details or the context

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:25.140
exactly, but suffice it to
say that just this past week,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.480
a controversy arose involving
a staff, some students, and one

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.850
or two faculty members.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.030
And President Arnn stepped
into the discussion

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.820
of this controversy
with an email.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.750
And I'll quote you from
the end of his email.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.740
Quote, "it is worth
saying, if too general,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.520
that the ultimate standard we
seek is [GREEK] this is a Greek

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.190
word--

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:55.760
like-mindedness that allows a
community to act in concord.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.040
For the sake of
this, Aristotle says,

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.490
it is not good enough
for people to be just,

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.990
while if they are friends,
there is no need to be just."

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.990
"So far, he continued, "this
sounds as though friendship

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:12.770
is merely something advantageous
for the social and political

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.210
good.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.270
But Aristotle immediately
adds that it is also

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.800
beautiful or noble.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.180
Like-mindedness is more than
justice, but it is not less.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.690
We need to have
both," end quote.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.620
So my first point is,
this is not typical email

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.680
from the president
of Hillsdale College,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.440
reflects the fact that he
is an atypical president.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.330
And my second point goes to the
difference between Washington

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:47.370
DC today and Hillsdale and that
is that unlike in Washington--

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.700
for those of us who have a hand
in managing Hillsdale College--

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.804
emails like this are
what we call, a stimulus.

00:02:56.804 --> 00:02:59.189
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.540
Please welcome, again, the 12th
president of Hillsdale College,

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.081
Dr. Larry Arnn.

00:03:07.081 --> 00:03:09.536
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.790
LARRY ARNN: Thank you, Doug.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:32.020
I did write that weird thing.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.930
See, I'm teaching
Aristotle this term.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.500
And so, if you're a hammer,
everything looks like a nail.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.520
I'm trying to remember--

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.960
what was the controversy
that led me to write that?

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.430
Probably had something to do
with the college newspaper.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.500
So I have some thanks to
say, is my main point,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.870
and then I have to frighten
you and then we'll be done.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:11.601
It's an unusual night because
a large number of my women folk

00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:12.100
are here.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.350
And it's also unusual
because there's

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.990
900 people or 850 or
something in this room

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:25.720
and then, there's a very large
number watching on TV-- hi.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.870
And because the great
majority of the people

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.090
are not going to be able
to see the women that I'm

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:33.645
going to mention, I can
describe them however I please.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.790
I'm married to an English
girl and her name is Penny.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.910
And her sister is here.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:50.020
And our younger
daughter, Alice, is here.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.770
And her sister, Jane, is
her name, and my mother,

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.590
Georgia-- now
deceased, and Alice

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:58.700
all have something in common.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.540
And because they're
two in her lineage--

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.930
Alice's-- that contribute this,
Alice is particularly that way.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.070
Alice is very stubborn.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.660
But also, they're
all very beautiful

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.690
and so, Penny and Jane and
Alice, please stand up.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.140
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.716
Turn around, turn
around, turn around.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.860
And to you people out
there, I want you to know,

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.560
none of the people in this
room think that Alice is good

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:37.530
looking because of me.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.091
I thank the speakers today.

00:05:47.091 --> 00:05:50.013
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.630
I think we're in the middle
of a long period of strife

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.750
and trouble and eventually, war.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:09.360
And I think that we're not
going to lose our country.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.940
And one of the
reasons I think that--

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.898
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.160
--is that God has provided
us people like Paul

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:20.780
Ryan and Michael Mukasey.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.020
And Tom West, and Doug
Jeffrey, and Paul Marino,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.750
and John Shattuck are
colleagues of mine,

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.937
and they're astonishingly good.

00:06:35.937 --> 00:06:36.770
And Martha Derthick.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:38.150
And I didn't get
to hear them today

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:39.983
because I had to go
over to the Kirby Center

00:06:39.983 --> 00:06:42.490
and show the Kirby
Center to Alan Kirby--

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.310
who liked it, I'm
happy to report.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.070
We're having this conference
because a choice is coming.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:07.140
We don't think that it's our
business to push or compel

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.390
anyone to do anything.

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.259
We believe in free government.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:21.300
But it does so happen,
that facts are facts,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.790
and especially, if one
understands the principles

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.980
according to which
they operate, you

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.990
can figure out what they mean.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.130
And I'll name you two.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:36.220
And in combination, they
mean danger and hope.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:42.100
Danger-- I read that last
week, the Department of Health

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.042
and Human Services--

00:07:44.042 --> 00:07:47.190
you ever seen that building?

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:47.830
It's ugly.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.980
And I favor the
architectural method

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:00.190
of constitutional reform.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:05.050
In this city, many things
operate in beautiful buildings

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:09.580
and many things operate
in ugly buildings.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.590
And one can tell which
is constitutional

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.023
by that distinction.

00:08:19.854 --> 00:08:22.270
And the Department of Health
and Human Services last week,

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:30.580
gave $700 million in grants to
state agencies and contractors

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.690
to begin the
implementation of what

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:38.190
we call the Patient Protection
and Affordable Care Act--

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.669
Obamacare.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:45.350
And think about
700 million bucks.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:50.030
That's a small city,
except full of people who

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:53.620
are very interested and smart.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:56.380
And they now are
enrolled in the job

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.640
of bringing that thing to be.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.460
And for that and for
Dodd-Frank, this week,

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.620
they're doing rulemaking--
and the next week,

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.244
and the week after,
and the week after.

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:08.270
And the rule making process
works in the most wonderful way

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:11.510
because what happens
is, they call a hearing

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:13.310
and they start working on it.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.970
And everybody, all the
various trade associations

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.610
and all the stuff that's
going on in this town--

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:24.260
and by the way, in 1960, there
was no Fortune 500 company

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.770
that had a telephone number
or an address in this town

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:29.529
because you didn't
need to be here.

00:09:29.529 --> 00:09:31.070
They were busy
governing the country,

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.550
not taking everybody's property.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:37.860
And so when you start the
rulemaking, what happens

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.170
is all these people
show up and they

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.970
want to get their
seat at the table

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.870
and so they all get
their chit to intervene

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:50.160
and be of influence, by agreeing
that the whole process is

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.900
legitimate and endorsing the
final outcome in advance.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:10:00.410
Now that's going on right
now, before the next election.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.510
And that means that even
after the next election,

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.992
it'll be very hard to
do anything about that.

00:10:05.992 --> 00:10:08.920
And I don't even need
to argue tonight--

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.570
and I probably don't need
to argue to any of you--

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.432
that that's not a good thing.

00:10:13.432 --> 00:10:14.890
The point that is
important to make

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:17.680
is that that's a
different way of doing it

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.810
than it used to be done.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.530
It is entirely different.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:27.930
And that there is a way, long
proved and long established,

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:31.530
now uprooted of governing
the country that

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:34.770
precedes by different methods.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:38.260
And those methods are--

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:40.450
since Doug read that memo--

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:44.192
my own theory is you govern
people best by goals.

00:10:44.192 --> 00:10:46.150
You have to have a very
clear line of authority

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:47.800
and you have to get
everybody to agree

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.260
to the same goal-- say the
Declaration of Independence

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:54.190
or [GREEK].

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.380
And then, watch them work.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.970
Its marvels unfold.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.220
And you have to intervene
once in a while, when you've

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.011
got something to say,
but mostly, they just

00:11:02.011 --> 00:11:06.140
do wonderful things and you
get to take credit for them.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.720
That's the constitutional
method of rule.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:14.780
And wherever it thrives,
everything thrives.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:18.150
And that's the
opposite way and that's

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:21.760
the gloomy thing
that is entrenched.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.340
And all over the world--

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.260
by the way-- while
we subject ourselves

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:30.120
to those in efficient methods
that waste our substance

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.780
and produce poor
results, our enemies

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:39.669
grow in power with every passing
day and we need to be strong.

00:11:39.669 --> 00:11:42.034
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:48.790
So that's the gloomy part.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:52.719
And then the happy part is,
how would we get strong?

00:11:52.719 --> 00:11:54.010
And I'll tell you a sign of it.

00:11:56.142 --> 00:11:58.100
I'm going to mention a
particular guy-- there's

00:11:58.100 --> 00:11:59.974
a bunch of congressmen
here tonight, I think,

00:11:59.974 --> 00:12:01.340
and this guy's not here.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:04.630
Shame on him, but I'm
going to praise him anyway

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.480
because he's great and
an old friend of mine.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.870
I got a call in the middle
of the debt ceiling debate

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.621
and I was watching
the thing and I

00:12:14.621 --> 00:12:16.370
didn't know what to
think about it exactly

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:21.680
because I had the honor never
to have been a legislator.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:25.670
And there's a congressman named
McClintock from California.

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.740
And a long time ago at
the Claremont Institute

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.448
where I used to work--
and Brian Kennedy,

00:12:29.448 --> 00:12:32.180
it's current president,
is here somewhere.

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:33.070
I think he's here.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:37.210
Anyway, I don't like him
because he used to work for me.

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:38.740
He's a really
great guy and I was

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:41.095
confident nobody could run
that place after I stopped.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:46.570
And it's just very angry
making to me that he can.

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.460
But McClintock used
to work with there

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.470
and he's a really great guy.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:57.220
And you know him?

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:58.850
You He's a really a great guy.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:04.290
And he's a sophomore congressman
from northern California.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:07.540
Going to help save the
country, he will do that.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:08.850
And one of our boys--

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.571
one of my boys, a
kid I had an class

00:13:10.571 --> 00:13:12.070
named Will Dunham--
very bright kid.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:14.260
He might be here tonight.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:18.780
But if he is, he says to sit way
in the back because he's young.

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:20.820
And Will called me
one day and he said,

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:25.260
do you think we're right
about the debt ceiling vote?

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:29.380
Tom was one of the 20
some votes against it.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:32.299
And I said, I don't know, I
haven't thought about it much.

00:13:32.299 --> 00:13:33.090
What are you doing?

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:34.170
And he said, we're
going to vote no.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.150
And I said, yeah, but what
if the country defaults

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:36.660
or something?

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:37.440
Won't that be bad?

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:41.040
And he said, yeah, I'm a
little worried about it.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.940
Tom's going to call you.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:44.820
And they're busy.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.324
They're voting on
a Thursday night.

00:13:47.324 --> 00:13:49.740
And it's coming down to the
short strokes-- it was finally

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.865
settled on that Sunday.

00:13:50.865 --> 00:13:52.740
And my phone rings in
a little while and it's

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:53.940
my friend, Tom, on the phone.

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:55.310
And he says, well,
how am I doing?

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.351
And I said, I don't know.

00:13:56.351 --> 00:13:57.280
What are you doing?

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.700
And he said, well, I'm
going to vote no on this.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.380
And I said, well, explain it.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:03.430
You got a good argument?

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:05.650
I said, anyway, Tom, you're
a legislator, I'm not.

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:07.025
You know more
about it than I do.

00:14:07.025 --> 00:14:09.395
And he said, "well, you
taught me everything I know.

00:14:09.395 --> 00:14:11.770
And I want you to understand
that phenomenon, by the way,

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:13.720
because I didn't.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.390
He knows more than I do
about a lot of things.

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:19.720
I taught him some things as some
people taught me those things.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:23.070
And I remember the
power of that bond,

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:28.070
and I'm talking about a man,
Tom, that I used to work with

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.050
and we learned some
things together.

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:35.450
And a man, Will, that I used to
have in class and we work with.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:37.620
And we learned some
things together.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:39.280
And we're friends.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:39.780
[GREEK]

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:44.670
And I said, so tell
me your argument.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:46.770
And he said, my
argument is simple.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:50.020
And I think you'll like
this when you hear it.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.060
He said that all of this
is a storm in a teacup

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:56.670
because it very often happens
every week, every month,

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.270
every year.

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:01.780
That something comes
up and something

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:08.470
has to be done for sure,
but the parties don't agree.

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:11.350
That happens all the time.

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:14.142
And there's a way of
dealing with it, he said.

00:15:14.142 --> 00:15:16.600
And the way is prescribed in
the Constitution of the United

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:17.800
States.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:22.780
We have a bicameral legislature
and separation of powers.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:26.920
And by the way, now this
kids telling me this

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.090
and it's repeating
back to me things.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:33.670
And he said, what we
do in a case like that

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.240
is that one house
passes its bill

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.730
and becomes
responsible before God

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.820
and the American people
for what it wants to do.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:45.520
And the other house
passes its bill

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.310
and becomes responsible before
God and the American people

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:49.780
for what it wants to do.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:52.210
And then everybody knows
what they want to do

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:55.420
and that empowers the
people to make a judgment.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:58.942
And then after that, they
have a conference committee.

00:15:58.942 --> 00:16:00.900
And the conference
committee makes three steps.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:03.980
And only three and it
always makes the same three.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:06.750
And the first one is, it
takes everything in both bills

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:09.110
and it puts them in a bill.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:13.160
And the second thing it does
is, it makes a strict judgment

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.230
about what else must
be added to the bill

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:18.002
to make a bill that works.

00:16:18.002 --> 00:16:19.460
And then the third
thing it does is

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.370
it takes things from
each bills, wherever

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.770
possible within the
parameters of the two bills,

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.110
within the extremes
of the two bills,

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:29.300
and it puts those
together to make a bill

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:31.640
and then both houses
get to vote on that.

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:34.970
And they get compromised
at the same time,

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.120
having had an
opportunity to stand

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:40.160
for what they stand for first.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:41.960
So we can all see it, you see.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.690
Because we need to
see it because we

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:50.200
need to judge because they
are our representatives.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:51.840
And for the three powers--

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.900
the president, and the speaker,
and the leader of the Senate--

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:59.400
to meet in the White House and
decide in advance what to do

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:03.550
is an obscuring of the things
that people need to know,

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:05.498
says my friend Tom McClintock.

00:17:05.498 --> 00:17:07.938
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:17.920
I, said Tom McClintock,
took an oath

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.980
and I will not participate
in such a thing.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:22.470
I will compromise.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:31.360
I know that it is
needful to compromise,

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:34.110
but I will not hide the facts
from the people of the United

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.520
States.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:42.190
Now, my point is that thing
is happening right now

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:44.120
in the Congress of
the United States

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.790
and Tom is not the only one.

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:49.870
And that means that we
might lose our country,

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:52.250
but we are not going to
do it without an argument.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:04.870
And in 1940, Churchill-- with
his photographic memory--

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:09.140
asked a young man-- just like my
kids, Will, and my kids, Kyle,

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:12.530
and my kids, Craig
who work around here.

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:16.480
John Colville was his name
later a knight, now dead.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:21.520
He said, John,
there's a prayer that

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:26.480
was made famous at the siege
of Gibraltar by the Spanish,

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:28.930
would you find it for me?

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:32.170
And he said, gladly, sir.

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:36.330
Fear not the result,
for either thy end shall

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.870
be a majestic and an
enviable one or God

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:44.290
will perpetuate our
rain upon the waters.

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:46.264
Let us save our country.

00:18:46.264 --> 00:18:48.724
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:14.790
DOUG JEFFREY: Well, our
main speaker tonight

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:18.240
is recently becoming,
it seems, a regular

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.970
at Hillsdale College events.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:26.160
One reason for this is that
he is undeniably brilliant.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.360
He earned a degree in--

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:29.735
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:35.830
He earned a degree in political
science at McGill University,

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:38.380
studied politics as a
commonwealth scholar

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:41.740
at Balliol College,
Oxford and earned a MD

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:44.230
from Harvard Medical School.

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:48.490
The deeper reason is
possibly, that as an MD,

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:52.430
his area of expertise
with psychiatry--

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:54.460
which means that he is
increasingly qualified

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:57.120
to comment on contemporary
American public policy.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.170
We know him and owe
him primarily today

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:09.780
for his Pulitzer Prize
winning syndicated column

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.540
for The Washington Post.

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:14.040
For his articles in journals,
such as The Weekly Standard

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:15.360
and The New Republic.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:18.330
And for his unsurpassed
commentary on the best TV news

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:21.027
show going, Fox
News Special Report.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:27.980
It's a great pleasure
to introduce him again

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:31.488
to a Hillsdale audience, please
welcome, Charles Krauthammer.

00:20:31.488 --> 00:20:33.933
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:44.944 --> 00:20:46.610
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER:
Thank you very much

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:48.860
for that kind introduction.

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:50.690
The definition of
a kind introduction

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:52.598
is when they leave stuff out.

00:20:52.598 --> 00:20:55.038
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:59.310
He left out that I was
once a speechwriter

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.105
for Walter Mondale.

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:08.240
I expected that reaction.

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:11.120
And I know what
you're all asking,

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:13.460
how do you go from Walter
Mondale to Fox News?

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:18.620
Answer is short and simple--

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:19.790
I was young once.

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:22.215
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:22.215 --> 00:21:24.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:33.320
Sat all the way in the back
with all the young people.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:40.690
But he did mention the
psychiatry part, which was

00:21:40.690 --> 00:21:46.670
not unkind, but not essential.

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:51.080
But it is true, I
was a psychiatrist.

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:52.940
I'm still licensed, actually.

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:53.930
I am a psychiatrist.

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:56.830
But the best way
to explain it is

00:21:56.830 --> 00:22:00.010
to say that I'm a
psychiatrist in remission.

00:22:02.976 --> 00:22:04.100
Doing very well, thank you.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.010
I haven't had a
relapse in 25 years.

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:12.940
People ask me the
difference between what

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:17.260
I used to do as a psychiatrist
in Boston 25 years ago,

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:19.990
from what I do now, as
a political analyst here

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:23.210
in Washington, and the
answer is rather simple.

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:25.240
In both lines of
work, i deal every day

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:28.620
with people who suffer
from delusions of grandeur.

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:41.810
The only difference is that here
in Washington, these deluded

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:47.020
have access to
nuclear weapons, which

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:50.620
makes the stakes a little
higher and the work

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:53.680
a lot more interesting.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:58.775
I'm really happy to be here
at another Hillsdale event.

00:22:58.775 --> 00:23:01.980
The reason is-- as you
know, as so many people

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:05.970
know-- you do extraordinary
work in your teaching,

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:11.430
in the institutions you support,
in the values you promote.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:14.340
Although, I have to admit that--

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:17.220
truth be told-- I'm happy
to be anywhere where Juan

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:18.730
Williams can't interrupt me.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:21.175
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:37.006
I'll tell him what kind of
reception he got [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:41.140
It will warm his heart.

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:49.120
Why do we celebrate
Constitution Day?

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:53.650
Let me start with an incident
that happened 30 years ago.

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:55.990
When Anwar Sadat
was assassinated,

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:59.230
the networks ran over
to Egypt and began

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:02.530
covering the events
all day and all night.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:05.700
And the only thing I
remember of all that coverage

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:11.770
it was a news anchor bringing
in a Middle East expert

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:16.790
and saying, we've just looked
at the Egyptian constitution.

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:20.350
And our researchers
tell us that the next

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:25.840
in line for the presidency is
the speaker of the Parliament.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:31.240
And the Middle East expert
burst out laughing, and said,

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:36.080
nobody in Egypt has read the
Constitution in 30 years.

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.420
No one knows it exists.

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:40.390
And no one cares what's in it.

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:42.760
He said, who's the
leader of the military?

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:45.040
Anchor says, Hosni Mubarak.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:48.540
And he says, he's
your next president.

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:52.240
What struck me about
that was two things.

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:57.570
First of all, how naive we are
about what constitutions are

00:24:57.570 --> 00:24:59.070
and what they mean
around the world.

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:03.150
And the second thing,
the reason for the first,

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:06.750
is how much reverence we
have in the United States

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:13.630
and in very few other
countries for this document.

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:16.440
There are many things
that are miraculous

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:18.510
about the Constitution.

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:21.930
The first is that
somehow on this edge

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:25.254
of the civilized
world 250 years ago,

00:25:25.254 --> 00:25:26.670
there could have
been a collection

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:31.270
of such political geniuses
to have actually written it.

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:35.280
The second miracle is the
actual substance of it.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.800
The way that these
people are drawing

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:41.190
from a Locke and Montesquieu,
and from the Greeks,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:46.230
created an extraordinary
political apparatus

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:49.290
that a quarter of
a millennium later,

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:54.960
works and has worked
with incredible success

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.300
over these many years.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:58.560
But the third miracle--

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:01.890
and the one that I think
we appreciate the least--

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:06.450
is that fact of the reverence
that we have for it.

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:10.440
This is as rare as
the other two elements

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:12.840
that I mentioned before.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.360
This reverence is
so deeply ingrained

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:17.610
that we don't even see it.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:22.080
We just think it's in
the air that we breathe.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.150
But it is extraordinarily rare.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:27.330
It exists in a handful of
countries in the world.

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:29.820
I remember when the
British de-colonized

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:32.940
Africa in the late
'50s and early '60s.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:34.710
And they had these
great ceremonies

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:38.640
transferring the power to the
local new institutions, which

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:42.540
are all established in
imitation of the British system.

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:45.660
And you'd see the governor
general arrive wearing the wig

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:50.268
and he would invest power
in the new prime minister,

00:26:50.268 --> 00:26:52.330
in the new high court.

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:56.310
And within six months, it
had all been swept away

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:58.390
because it was artificial.

00:26:58.390 --> 00:27:00.120
It was not intrinsic.

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:03.300
And there was no reverence
for those institutions.

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:05.400
It takes time.

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:08.100
In Britain, it's been
around for eight centuries

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.890
and they manage a constitution
that's actually written,

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:15.270
which is even more
difficult than what we do.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:19.860
But here it is ingrained in
the consciences of the people.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:23.310
Consider the oath of office
that we take for granted.

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:26.520
And I remember doing
this 30 years ago

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:30.540
and how moved I was by
the words and by the idea.

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:33.300
Whenever we bestow upon
anyone the authority

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:37.680
to wield the power of
state over free citizens,

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:42.330
we make him swear to protect not
the people, not the nation, not

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:46.860
the flag, but the Constitution
of the United States-- a piece

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:48.870
of paper.

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:52.950
Of course, it stands for the
pillars of the American idea,

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:56.670
the structures, the philosophy
that define limited government

00:27:56.670 --> 00:28:02.160
with enumerated powers whose
mission is to preserve liberty

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:04.510
and individual rights.

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:06.555
But this is a gift
that we have that.

00:28:06.555 --> 00:28:10.620
That people intrinsically
have this sense of reverence

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:12.840
for the Constitution.

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:16.890
And it's important to remember
that it's a gift from the past.

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:21.900
It's not something that we can
in any way credit to ourselves.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:25.980
If anything, our generation,
recent generations

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:28.590
have allowed that kind
of reverence to diminish,

00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:32.600
to bleed away over the
decades as we try--

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:38.940
as it were-- to adapt
constitutionalism to modernity.

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:43.440
This is, in particular,
a liberal specialty.

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:45.660
This idea of the
living, breathing,

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:52.230
ever-changing, ever-whimsical
new Constitution day-by-day.

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:59.180
And that is why the new
conservative idea is now

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:04.730
focused on the kind of
revival of constitutionalism.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:09.260
We can almost say that
the new conservatism

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:12.650
is, in essence, a
constitutionalism,

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.620
in and of itself.

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:19.160
First, let's start
with a bit of history.

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:23.030
In the 20th century, liberalism
outgrew its 19th century

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:24.920
roots-- the classic
individualism

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:27.440
of John Stuart Mill and others.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:32.360
And it fell into the thrall of
the progressivism of the age.

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:37.580
Mill held that truth emerges
from a competition of ideas

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:41.180
and that individual character
is most improved when

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:45.350
allowed to find its own way
unfettered by government,

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.740
when allowed to develop
with government standing

00:29:48.740 --> 00:29:50.660
to the side.

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:55.610
But that vision was
insufficient for the ambitions

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:58.790
of 20th century liberalism.

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:01.880
It lacked glory and
it lacked sweep.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:06.590
20th century liberalism new
found perfectionist ambitions

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:10.230
reflected in its current name--

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:13.790
progressivism-- sought
to harness the power

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:15.500
of government, the
mystique of science,

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:19.070
and the rule of experts to
shape society and individual

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:22.330
character and bring them both--

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:25.160
willing or not-- to a
higher state of being.

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:29.960
Contemporary conservative
is a reaction

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:33.410
to precisely that kind of
overreaching, overarching

00:30:33.410 --> 00:30:34.950
ambition.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:39.380
It's deeply skeptical of a
belief in progressive history

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:42.140
or redemptive politics.

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:44.570
It believes that the
first duty of government

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:46.310
is to conserve what is.

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:48.770
And most especially,
the great gift

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:52.610
of the Enlightenment, the
autonomy of the individual,

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:56.090
and the essence
of civil society--

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:59.390
what [INAUDIBLE] called,
the little platoons--

00:30:59.390 --> 00:31:03.380
that are created beneath,
against, and apart

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:06.626
from the behemoth of government.

00:31:06.626 --> 00:31:09.056
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:16.830
This is why the
conservative choice

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:19.890
among all the great
revolutions is always

00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:24.570
the American Revolution with
its constitutional structures,

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:28.020
limits, and
containment of power,

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:31.920
the checks and balances that
frustrate tyranny, and the view

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:36.750
that government is subordinate
to the individuals from whom

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:38.250
it derives its power.

00:31:41.612 --> 00:31:46.680
The conservative instant is
skeptical and individualistic.

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:51.280
And in the American
context, constitutional.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:53.200
What's so remarkable
about this is

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:58.060
that constitutions are
extremely reactionary documents.

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:01.240
The very essence of a
constitution is to constrain

00:32:01.240 --> 00:32:09.100
the enthusiasms of a future
that one cannot even see.

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:12.040
In America, constitutionalism
demands that even

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:16.420
the most distant
progeny swear allegiance

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:21.410
to a past embodied
in a document that

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:26.340
was written in the late 1780s.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:30.140
If, as Chesterton said,
tradition is the democracy

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:33.890
of the dead, then
constitutionalism--

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:37.460
which is wisdom rendered
into legal code--

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:39.080
is the tyranny of the dead.

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:44.390
The ultimate reach of
the past into the future.

00:32:44.390 --> 00:32:48.760
And in America, it succeeded.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:52.720
Lincoln Steffens--
the propagandist--

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:57.880
famously said after visiting
Bolshevik Russia shortly

00:32:57.880 --> 00:33:02.320
after the revolution, I
have been over to the future

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:05.410
and it works.

00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:12.280
American constitutionalism
declares, we have seen the past

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:13.180
and it works.

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:23.910
Which is, again, paradoxical
because we Americans--

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:26.430
the most forward
looking, blue sky,

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:31.650
futuristic people
on the planet who

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:37.830
have developed this system that
looks back to the past, that

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:42.120
reaches from the past into
the future to constrain us,

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.640
and have produced the
astonishing stability

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:49.260
of the American
experiment which owes

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:54.090
its power, and its
stability, and its majesty

00:33:54.090 --> 00:34:00.810
to the inherent restraint
of the original blueprint.

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:04.560
Compare the American Revolution
and its prudential institution

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:07.620
building with the
near contemporaneous

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:11.040
French Revolution,
which was the apotheosis

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:13.440
of political romanticism.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:16.900
With its worship of
reason and abstraction,

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:21.659
it is no accident that France
is on its fifth republic

00:34:21.659 --> 00:34:23.489
and we are on our first.

00:34:23.489 --> 00:34:25.884
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:37.340
But we don't have to look
that far back into history

00:34:37.340 --> 00:34:41.510
to understand why we
celebrate Constitution Day.

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:46.790
Consider what has been happening
in our contemporary world.

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:48.530
Today, Americans
are in the midst

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:52.310
of a great national debate
over the power, the scope,

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:55.070
and the reach of
government that was

00:34:55.070 --> 00:34:58.460
established by this document.

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:04.370
The debate was sparked by this
administration's bold push

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:09.170
for a government expansion,
a massive fiscal stimulus,

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:12.140
with apparently, a second
attempt at son of stimulus

00:35:12.140 --> 00:35:12.770
right now.

00:35:16.430 --> 00:35:20.640
Obamacare, the
financial regulation,

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:24.470
various attempts at
controlling the energy economy,

00:35:24.470 --> 00:35:29.150
and otherwise, regulating
the private and economic life

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:31.940
of Americans.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:35.450
This engendered a popular
reaction, but let me say,

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:37.610
before I mention the reaction--

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:40.020
the way to best understand
this, of course,

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:43.250
is to see Obama and the
vision he has, as someone

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:47.560
who wants to move America from
its traditional constitutional

00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:51.800
and restrained individualistic
system to a system

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:55.790
more like the social
democracy of Europe.

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:59.030
I think he's been rather
open and honest about that

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:01.140
and for that, I give him credit.

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:02.900
He failed to plan--

00:36:02.900 --> 00:36:06.440
very openly speaking-- in
those terms of changing America

00:36:06.440 --> 00:36:11.090
fundamentally within a month
of his extension to office.

00:36:11.090 --> 00:36:14.750
The best way to
understand this vision

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:18.500
is a story famously told
about Winston Churchill

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:21.810
shortly after he
lost the election

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:24.980
during or at the very end
of the Second World War.

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:26.860
And Clement Attlee
the socialist,

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:28.200
became the prime minister.

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:30.950
Well, Churchill was, of course,
the leader of the opposition

00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:35.270
and he went down to the men's
room at the House of Commons--

00:36:35.270 --> 00:36:36.740
that's as risque as it gets.

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:39.650
It's OK from now on.

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:42.390
And he sees Attlee standing
in one of the urinals.

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:45.420
Now, the men's room
is empty, otherwise,

00:36:45.420 --> 00:36:48.630
and yet, Churchill deliberately
walks to the other end

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:50.240
of the men's room--

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:51.870
18 stalls away.

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:53.920
And Attlee is rather surprised.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:56.490
And he calls out and
says, feeling a bit

00:36:56.490 --> 00:36:59.550
standoffish today, Winston?

00:36:59.550 --> 00:37:02.790
Churchill said, no,
my dear, Clement.

00:37:02.790 --> 00:37:05.940
It's just that any time
you see something large,

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.797
you want to nationalize it.

00:37:07.797 --> 00:37:10.282
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:25.900
I like it as much as you do.

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:30.530
No matter how many
times I tell it.

00:37:33.560 --> 00:37:37.375
So on the one side,
we have American hyper

00:37:37.375 --> 00:37:39.820
liberal government
trying to push us

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:42.430
to a more social
democratic system.

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:46.380
And then, miraculously or
astonishingly-- however

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:47.210
you want to see.

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:49.330
You can believe in
providence or not--

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:52.270
but amazingly, there's
a spontaneous reaction.

00:37:52.270 --> 00:37:53.740
It was not a lead.

00:37:53.740 --> 00:37:55.020
It was not organized.

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:56.080
There was no conspiracy.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:38:01.300
People reacted against
this push to the left.

00:38:01.300 --> 00:38:03.670
And the movement has
been called Tea Party,

00:38:03.670 --> 00:38:06.580
but in reality, it's
much more widespread.

00:38:06.580 --> 00:38:08.860
Expressed itself in
the November elections

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:12.520
of last year, which calls
for a more restricted vision

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:16.420
of government, more
consistent with the intent

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:17.635
and the aim of the founders.

00:38:26.010 --> 00:38:28.890
I would call it
constitutionalism or a return

00:38:28.890 --> 00:38:30.780
to constitutionalism.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:33.720
And what's interesting is that,
in essence, constitutionalism

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:38.130
is the intellectual counterpart
and the spiritual progeny

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:44.580
of the originalism movement
that we see in the law.

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:46.950
Judicial originalists--
for example,

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:50.600
Antonin Scalia and other
notable conservative jurists--

00:38:50.600 --> 00:38:53.150
insist-- yes, go ahead.

00:38:53.150 --> 00:38:55.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:58.060 --> 00:39:00.310
Wouldn't it be nice if
he ran for president?

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:07.520
But I digress.

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:10.370
These judicial
originalists insist

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:14.540
that legal interpretation be
bound by something, namely,

00:39:14.540 --> 00:39:16.850
the text of the
Constitution as understood

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:19.970
by those who wrote it
and their contemporaries.

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:24.470
Originalism originally
skewing as a kind of a fringe

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:28.220
tendency-- has now grown to
become the major challenger

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:31.760
to the liberal living
constitution school,

00:39:31.760 --> 00:39:34.880
under which high
court is the channeler

00:39:34.880 --> 00:39:38.180
of the spirit of the
age, free to create

00:39:38.180 --> 00:39:40.940
new constitutional
principles that

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:45.500
occur to people in dark robes.

00:39:45.500 --> 00:39:48.050
What originalism is
to jurisprudence,

00:39:48.050 --> 00:39:50.910
constitutionalism
is to governance--

00:39:50.910 --> 00:39:55.670
a call for restraint rooted
in constitutional text.

00:39:55.670 --> 00:39:59.230
Constitutionalism as a
political philosophy,

00:39:59.230 --> 00:40:03.770
represents a reformed self-m
regulating conservatism

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:07.250
that bases its call for
minimalist government

00:40:07.250 --> 00:40:10.782
for reigning in willful
presidents and congresses,

00:40:10.782 --> 00:40:12.740
in the words and the
meaning of a constitution.

00:40:12.740 --> 00:40:16.850
Now what does this mean
in practical terms?

00:40:16.850 --> 00:40:20.300
Let me give an
example to contrast

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:25.820
constitutional conservatism
with its nearest predecessor--

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:29.450
the compassionate
variety of George Bush.

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:32.160
Its heart wasn't
in the right place,

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:36.770
but that compassionate
conservatism failed.

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:40.160
Not just for practical reasons
because of profligate spending

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.640
and expansion of
government, but because

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:47.030
of its philosophical
inadequacies.

00:40:47.030 --> 00:40:51.530
First, its very name implied
that other conservatives

00:40:51.530 --> 00:40:56.270
are not compassionate, conceding
the central liberal premise

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:59.150
that small government
and belief in markets

00:40:59.150 --> 00:41:03.260
is hard-hearted rather
than being a philosophy.

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:07.130
That it is better suited
to organizing society

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:10.040
and among other things,
helping the poor

00:41:10.040 --> 00:41:11.515
and the disadvantaged
to flourish.

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:21.190
The second problem with that
variety of conservative thought

00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:23.120
is that it believes
in small government,

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:27.370
except when the president
wakes up on one side of the bed

00:41:27.370 --> 00:41:32.650
and decides it's time for
a prescription drug benefit

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:35.980
or a massive program
for African AIDS.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:37.690
Now I have no
particular objection

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:40.870
to either of these
initiatives, and I

00:41:40.870 --> 00:41:43.480
don't mean to be as
dismissive as I sounded.

00:41:43.480 --> 00:41:47.560
I really did sound dismissive
of these initiatives.

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:49.330
But here's the problem.

00:41:49.330 --> 00:41:52.510
Why AIDS and not
river blindness?

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:57.070
Why Africa and not the
scourges of the poor precincts

00:41:57.070 --> 00:41:59.200
of South America or Asia?

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:03.280
Why prescription drugs for
seniors or not for children?

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:05.890
It is very undisciplined
and arbitrary

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:10.630
and that's why it fails as
a philosophical proposition,

00:42:10.630 --> 00:42:13.280
as well as having all the
practical difficulties that I

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:16.930
mentioned of encouraging
rather arbitrarily

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:20.830
and engendering a large
expansion of government.

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:24.070
The new constitutionalism
is a kind

00:42:24.070 --> 00:42:27.130
of self-enforced discipline.

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:31.750
And it is held by consciously
grounding itself in the text--

00:42:31.750 --> 00:42:33.670
constitutional text.

00:42:33.670 --> 00:42:35.950
The first symbolic
moment of that

00:42:35.950 --> 00:42:39.700
occurred in January, when the
new House of Representatives

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:42.520
opened with a reading
of the Constitution.

00:42:42.520 --> 00:42:45.280
Remarkably, this
had never been done

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:49.600
before in American history, but
perhaps, because it had never

00:42:49.600 --> 00:42:52.720
been so needed.

00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:56.200
The reading reflected the
feeling expressed powerfully

00:42:56.200 --> 00:42:59.860
in the last election,
that we had moved far--

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:02.410
especially in the
last two years--

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:04.330
from a government
of constitutionally

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:08.170
limited and enumerated powers
to the direction of government

00:43:08.170 --> 00:43:14.320
constrained only by its
perception of social need.

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:17.800
The most galvanizing example
of this expansive shift

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:22.580
was, of course, the
democratic health care reform,

00:43:22.580 --> 00:43:27.710
which was and might yet one
day, if it's not repealed,

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:32.660
revolutionize one sixth
of the American economy.

00:43:32.660 --> 00:43:37.000
But note the nature of the
popular reaction against it.

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:39.710
The most interesting and
encouraging part of the push

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:45.250
back against this kind of
expansion of big government

00:43:45.250 --> 00:43:47.290
was the form it took.

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:51.130
There was, of course, the usual
opposition on the grounds--

00:43:51.130 --> 00:43:56.740
the usual grounds-- of objecting
to welfare state expansion.

00:43:56.740 --> 00:43:58.990
That it's ruinously expensive.

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:01.120
It's unsustainable economically.

00:44:01.120 --> 00:44:05.410
It introduces vast
inefficiency and arbitrariness

00:44:05.410 --> 00:44:08.710
as we see in all the waivers
that HHS is granting.

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:11.410
Again, arbitrarily that
would be a system that

00:44:11.410 --> 00:44:14.290
would be so complicated
and so unwilling

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:18.310
that it would degrade the
entire medical system itself,

00:44:18.310 --> 00:44:25.240
as well as contributing to our
looming national insolvency.

00:44:25.240 --> 00:44:27.040
Now, that kind of protest--

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:29.010
all of those arguments--

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:30.580
would have been the
norm and we might

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:32.440
have stopped there
in preceding years,

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:34.400
but it didn't stop there.

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:36.580
This time there was
an additional argument

00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:39.700
that arose and became
very powerful--

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:41.970
constitutional illegitimacy.

00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:53.680
And that objection manifested
itself in two forms--

00:44:53.680 --> 00:44:57.430
Popular opposition and political
argument on the one hand,

00:44:57.430 --> 00:45:01.420
as well as a serious legal
challenge on the other.

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:06.010
The object of the aversion on
the part of the conservatives

00:45:06.010 --> 00:45:10.570
was, of course, the heart
of this monstrosity--

00:45:10.570 --> 00:45:12.650
the individual mandate.

00:45:12.650 --> 00:45:14.450
The requirement by
the federal government

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:17.200
that every citizen
buy health insurance

00:45:17.200 --> 00:45:19.510
from a private entity
under the penalty

00:45:19.510 --> 00:45:21.940
of a fine from Washington.

00:45:21.940 --> 00:45:23.980
Now, from town
hall to town hall,

00:45:23.980 --> 00:45:25.930
from campaign
debates to arguments

00:45:25.930 --> 00:45:28.720
on the floor of the
Congress, people

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:31.360
felt and saw this
as, instinctively,

00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:33.420
a bridge too far.

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:36.040
That on principle,
even if Obamacare

00:45:36.040 --> 00:45:39.970
was economical,
beneficence, and efficient,

00:45:39.970 --> 00:45:43.540
this would be grounds enough
to declare it impermissible.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:46.750
Impermissible to compel a
citizen to do something,

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:49.360
to do something positive.

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:52.150
Not just omit to do
something, but to do something

00:45:52.150 --> 00:45:55.470
under compulsion, simply to
promote what the government saw

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:58.390
as some social good.

00:45:58.390 --> 00:46:00.670
Interestingly, it
spawned a legal challenge

00:46:00.670 --> 00:46:03.250
that, at first, was
dismissed by the better

00:46:03.250 --> 00:46:08.360
thinkers in this town as simply,
the work of fringe elements--

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:11.000
something to be dismissed.

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:14.410
And the Democrats were
extremely dismissive

00:46:14.410 --> 00:46:19.330
of this constitutional path to
object to it at the beginning.

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:23.080
Yet within several months,
the legal challenge

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:26.800
was joined by a majority
of the 50 states.

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:29.830
The basis of the argument
was that government has

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:32.890
exceeded its enumerated power.

00:46:32.890 --> 00:46:36.070
Now, I want to make one
point about how refreshing

00:46:36.070 --> 00:46:39.360
that line of argument is.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:41.050
It's a subtle one,
but I think it's

00:46:41.050 --> 00:46:45.580
important to
understand and to see.

00:46:45.580 --> 00:46:49.210
The traditional defense
against government encroachment

00:46:49.210 --> 00:46:52.120
over the last two
decades has been

00:46:52.120 --> 00:46:55.930
not to argue about
enumerated powers,

00:46:55.930 --> 00:47:00.710
but to argue on the grounds
of the Bill of Rights

00:47:00.710 --> 00:47:05.170
and to claim individual rights
in the inviolable sphere

00:47:05.170 --> 00:47:08.410
of freedom and sovereignty
of the individual.

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:12.390
You can't do X because it
goes against individual rights

00:47:12.390 --> 00:47:14.560
and the Bill of Rights.

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:17.500
But that idea, that
kind of defense

00:47:17.500 --> 00:47:22.450
tends to concede that outside of
that private sphere surrounding

00:47:22.450 --> 00:47:29.890
the individual, the government
is free to roam and to rule.

00:47:29.890 --> 00:47:34.360
The attack today on the
basis of enumerated powers

00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:38.200
is a stronger attack
on big government.

00:47:38.200 --> 00:47:41.920
Because now, it argues
that it is government, not

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:43.840
the individual,
that is constrained

00:47:43.840 --> 00:47:46.570
by a sphere around it.

00:47:46.570 --> 00:47:50.500
And that sphere
constrains the government

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:54.580
because of the enumerated
powers in the Constitution

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:56.830
beyond which it may not go.

00:48:04.910 --> 00:48:08.630
So you limit government
to that sphere

00:48:08.630 --> 00:48:12.530
and that means that everything
else outside of it, which

00:48:12.530 --> 00:48:18.830
is everything else in life,
is the sovereign domain

00:48:18.830 --> 00:48:22.040
of the individual
end of civil society.

00:48:22.040 --> 00:48:24.320
It's kind of a distinction
between the figure

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:26.180
and the ground.

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:28.760
If you focus on
enumerated power,

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:32.750
the ultimate objective is
to restrain the government,

00:48:32.750 --> 00:48:36.170
to keep it in a box
and everything else,

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:37.550
it cannot touch.

00:48:37.550 --> 00:48:40.070
Rather than the
traditional defense

00:48:40.070 --> 00:48:42.760
against encroachment
of government, which is

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:44.030
I'm the individual.

00:48:44.030 --> 00:48:46.070
You draw a circle around me.

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:49.340
You can't penetrate it, but
everything outside of it,

00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:50.450
is yours.

00:48:50.450 --> 00:48:52.700
In some ways, it's a
kind of a recapitulation

00:48:52.700 --> 00:48:55.490
of the argument on the
basis of the 10th Amendment,

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:56.532
but it has--

00:48:56.532 --> 00:48:58.490
I think-- a larger
implication because once you

00:48:58.490 --> 00:49:00.680
talk about enumerated
powers, you're

00:49:00.680 --> 00:49:04.250
going to the heart of the
expansion of the state

00:49:04.250 --> 00:49:06.770
ever since the New Deal.

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:10.230
And that, I think, is
why it's so important.

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:13.680
The challenge to the individual
mandate is not, at its heart,

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:18.210
a claim of one or
another individual right

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:20.550
under the guarantees
in the Bill of Rights.

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:23.460
The argument is that the
use of the Commerce Clause

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:25.050
to compel the
individual to enter

00:49:25.050 --> 00:49:28.410
into a private contract
in the name of promoting

00:49:28.410 --> 00:49:30.750
interstate commerce
is stretching

00:49:30.750 --> 00:49:35.290
the clause beyond
recognition, beyond reason.

00:49:35.290 --> 00:49:38.820
And to the point where there
would be no conceivable limit

00:49:38.820 --> 00:49:42.120
to the indefinite and infinite
expansion of government power.

00:49:51.010 --> 00:49:53.290
And the conclusion of
that line of argument

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:57.400
is that if you allow
this, how can you possibly

00:49:57.400 --> 00:50:00.850
prohibit government from
doing anything in the future

00:50:00.850 --> 00:50:05.200
that it seeks to promote
a preferred policy option?

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:09.340
This-- to me-- is the
frontal judicial attack

00:50:09.340 --> 00:50:10.960
on big government.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:13.690
The Commerce Clause,
for 80 years,

00:50:13.690 --> 00:50:17.950
has been the high road to the
expansion of government, which

00:50:17.950 --> 00:50:20.200
is why the framers
of Obamacare were

00:50:20.200 --> 00:50:22.720
so contemptuous and
dismissive at the beginning

00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:25.720
of any constitutional challenge.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:29.470
That they'd just assume
we've been using it ever

00:50:29.470 --> 00:50:32.530
since the '30s and we're
going to use it again.

00:50:32.530 --> 00:50:35.080
And now it's under challenge
and these Democrats

00:50:35.080 --> 00:50:36.391
are not dismissive anymore.

00:50:36.391 --> 00:50:37.015
They're scared.

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:47.820
They're afraid of two things.

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:51.740
That they will be rebuked
and defeated by the courts.

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:56.540
And even if not, that they
will be rebuked and defeated

00:50:56.540 --> 00:50:58.010
by the people at the polls.

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:12.500
That's why I have reasonable
hope for the future.

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:18.190
I do think that this popular
reaction-- again, incohaint,

00:51:18.190 --> 00:51:20.950
unorganized, undirected--

00:51:20.950 --> 00:51:24.280
that developed into this tsunami
that we saw on election day

00:51:24.280 --> 00:51:27.780
last year and that still
animates the opposition,

00:51:27.780 --> 00:51:31.180
is one unbelievably
wondrous sign

00:51:31.180 --> 00:51:34.240
of the health of the
body of politics.

00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:37.000
And the fact that
it has concentrated

00:51:37.000 --> 00:51:39.730
on exactly the correct
constitutional issues

00:51:39.730 --> 00:51:41.800
that it refers to.

00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:46.000
Finds its strength in
constitutionalism itself.

00:51:46.000 --> 00:51:46.910
Is encouraging.

00:51:46.910 --> 00:51:49.690
It's not just the
traditional arguments

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:54.100
that Obamacare or these other
expansions are inefficient.

00:51:54.100 --> 00:51:56.830
They are not economically sound.

00:51:56.830 --> 00:51:58.900
They lead to
bureaucratic efficiency.

00:51:58.900 --> 00:52:01.540
That would be OK, but
it wouldn't be enough,

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:03.370
not at this time.

00:52:03.370 --> 00:52:08.770
The argument now, the
resistance now is emphasizing,

00:52:08.770 --> 00:52:14.740
is rooted in an attack on the
constitutional illegitimacy

00:52:14.740 --> 00:52:16.360
of what is being done.

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:19.330
And that in a
constitutional republic

00:52:19.330 --> 00:52:20.590
is the heart of the matter.

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:32.830
This does not in any way
denigrate the other kinds

00:52:32.830 --> 00:52:35.740
of conservative critique
of mine and liberalism,

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:37.960
but it serves to reinforce it.

00:52:37.960 --> 00:52:43.090
In choosing to focus on a
majestic document that compares

00:52:43.090 --> 00:52:45.700
both study and
recitation, this kind

00:52:45.700 --> 00:52:49.480
of reformed conservatism
of the Obama era

00:52:49.480 --> 00:52:53.920
has found not just a
symbol, but an anchor.

00:52:53.920 --> 00:52:56.560
Constitutionalism as
a guiding tendency

00:52:56.560 --> 00:52:59.590
will require careful and
thoughtful development,

00:52:59.590 --> 00:53:05.590
just as its counterpart in
jurisprudence originalism

00:53:05.590 --> 00:53:09.160
has required careful
thought and development.

00:53:09.160 --> 00:53:13.000
But its very existence of
the power of this critique

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:17.230
and the popular and
spiritual support it receives

00:53:17.230 --> 00:53:22.180
is reason for hope,
if not for change.

00:53:22.180 --> 00:53:23.606
Thank you very much.

00:53:23.606 --> 00:53:26.086
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:03.810 --> 00:54:06.240
I believe we're going
to have about a quarter

00:54:06.240 --> 00:54:08.700
hour of questions and
answers, but before you

00:54:08.700 --> 00:54:13.290
ask any questions, let me
just say that if nominated,

00:54:13.290 --> 00:54:16.610
I will not run.

00:54:16.610 --> 00:54:19.530
However, if elected,
I will serve.

00:54:25.880 --> 00:54:27.060
I'm just lazy.

00:54:27.060 --> 00:54:30.030
I don't want to bother.

00:54:30.030 --> 00:54:33.080
So I think we've got
microphones-- do we set up?

00:54:33.080 --> 00:54:35.890
Or do I have to ask
myself questions?

00:54:35.890 --> 00:54:38.390
SPEAKER: We only have time for
three questions this evening,

00:54:38.390 --> 00:54:41.097
and please, wait to
ask your question

00:54:41.097 --> 00:54:43.085
until you get the microphone.

00:54:48.277 --> 00:54:49.860
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER:
Three questions--

00:54:49.860 --> 00:54:51.795
it sounds like a
merchant of Venice.

00:54:54.980 --> 00:54:59.750
Three boxes-- yes.

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:02.364
We're having a debate
over who gets to ask it.

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:08.360
AUDIENCE: Who's first?

00:55:08.360 --> 00:55:10.160
Here I am.

00:55:10.160 --> 00:55:12.275
First of all, I want to
tell you that I honestly

00:55:12.275 --> 00:55:15.690
believe that our
founding fathers were

00:55:15.690 --> 00:55:17.960
a miracle for this country.

00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:21.390
And when I look at somebody like
you, I think, you know what?

00:55:21.390 --> 00:55:22.140
They still happen.

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:23.559
Thank you.

00:55:23.559 --> 00:55:24.980
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: Thank you.

00:55:24.980 --> 00:55:26.686
AUDIENCE: My question is this--

00:55:30.040 --> 00:55:31.820
this is absolutely basic--

00:55:31.820 --> 00:55:34.050
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:55:34.050 --> 00:55:35.805
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: Yes.

00:55:35.805 --> 00:55:38.290
AUDIENCE: That's all.

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:40.290
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: I'm
not much of a believer,

00:55:40.290 --> 00:55:42.300
but there's something
about American history

00:55:42.300 --> 00:55:46.200
that truly makes you think that
there is a providential hand.

00:55:46.200 --> 00:55:48.150
Starting with the
Constitution, the fact

00:55:48.150 --> 00:55:52.530
that this unbelievable
assembly of geniuses could have

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:54.960
found themselves in
one place in one time

00:55:54.960 --> 00:55:56.340
at the edge of the known world.

00:55:56.340 --> 00:55:58.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:04.620
And the other part of this
about American history

00:56:04.620 --> 00:56:06.550
is astonishing to me.

00:56:06.550 --> 00:56:10.500
Apart from the fact
that Jefferson and Adams

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:14.730
died on the same day exactly
50 years after the signing

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:16.500
of the Declaration--

00:56:16.500 --> 00:56:19.260
what are the odds on that?

00:56:19.260 --> 00:56:21.730
I know it's only a
superstition on my part.

00:56:21.730 --> 00:56:24.690
But the other serious
part is the fact

00:56:24.690 --> 00:56:27.960
that our history
has always given us,

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:31.320
at the time it needed,
the leader it needed.

00:56:33.870 --> 00:56:36.150
It's miraculous that
when we needed a Lincoln,

00:56:36.150 --> 00:56:37.890
we got a Lincoln.

00:56:37.890 --> 00:56:43.590
And we may have difficulties
with FDR's domestic policy,

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:47.070
but when we needed that to
win the war and somebody who

00:56:47.070 --> 00:56:49.680
prepared us for that war in
a country that did not want

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:52.110
to go to war, he was there.

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:54.968
Then we had Reagan
when we needed him.

00:56:54.968 --> 00:57:03.740
Now, I don't see or expect or
wait for the next great figure,

00:57:03.740 --> 00:57:06.240
but I do think-- the one
thing I mentioned earlier,

00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:10.430
this spontaneous reaction to
overreaching by government,

00:57:10.430 --> 00:57:13.700
which nobody had predicted,
and manifested itself

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:16.330
so extraordinarily
over the last two years

00:57:16.330 --> 00:57:18.640
is another sign of hope.

00:57:18.640 --> 00:57:22.490
I'll leave it to you to decide
whether it's providential

00:57:22.490 --> 00:57:26.900
or not, but it is what makes you
think that America will endure.

00:57:26.900 --> 00:57:28.610
Absolutely.

00:57:28.610 --> 00:57:31.085
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:37.429 --> 00:57:38.845
Do we have a
question on the left?

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:42.980
Yes, Juan.

00:57:48.607 --> 00:57:52.370
AUDIENCE: Well, I'm a great
supporter of Hillsdale--

00:57:55.681 --> 00:57:56.627
stand up?

00:57:59.470 --> 00:58:03.170
And I think one of
the greatest things

00:58:03.170 --> 00:58:07.330
is when you have an
educational system that

00:58:07.330 --> 00:58:11.330
puts up with all of
us and encourages us,

00:58:11.330 --> 00:58:14.150
that our future is
going to be great.

00:58:14.150 --> 00:58:18.020
And I want to thank
you and the editors

00:58:18.020 --> 00:58:21.773
for letting us all come
and enjoy you Thanks.

00:58:26.490 --> 00:58:28.960
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: That
was very generous of you,

00:58:28.960 --> 00:58:31.980
and I won't count it as a
question so we've got two left.

00:58:36.070 --> 00:58:38.280
AUDIENCE: Sir, you've answered--

00:58:38.280 --> 00:58:41.680
to you right, the other
right-- there we go.

00:58:41.680 --> 00:58:42.777
You have answered--

00:58:42.777 --> 00:58:44.235
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER:
The far right.

00:58:44.235 --> 00:58:46.550
AUDIENCE: Far right, thank you.

00:58:46.550 --> 00:58:50.000
You and the other speakers
have answered all my questions,

00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:51.790
so I would only
like to point out

00:58:51.790 --> 00:58:56.470
that your discourse was
interrupted by far more

00:58:56.470 --> 00:58:59.970
frequently by applause than
our current sitting president

00:58:59.970 --> 00:59:00.883
and I thank you.

00:59:06.100 --> 00:59:09.910
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: But I would
attribute that to my eloquence,

00:59:09.910 --> 00:59:11.430
I had the much easier argument.

00:59:17.780 --> 00:59:21.200
I guess we're down
to the last one.

00:59:21.200 --> 00:59:21.740
Two more?

00:59:21.740 --> 00:59:24.260
All right.

00:59:24.260 --> 00:59:28.510
You're sovereign in this
room, that's for sure.

00:59:28.510 --> 00:59:30.695
AUDIENCE: Now that we're
at the 10th anniversary

00:59:30.695 --> 00:59:32.640
of the September
11th attacks, I was

00:59:32.640 --> 00:59:35.730
wondering if thought it had
been investigated well enough.

00:59:35.730 --> 00:59:38.640
And if you think it would be
constitutionally advisable

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:41.640
to restore the House Un-American
Activities Committee,

00:59:41.640 --> 00:59:43.520
especially since
there are riots--

00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:47.310
I believe-- planned
this Saturday, as well?

00:59:47.310 --> 00:59:50.800
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: The
answer to that is no.

00:59:50.800 --> 00:59:55.100
I don't believe in conspiracy
theories for several reasons.

00:59:55.100 --> 00:59:58.260
The facetious one
is that whenever

00:59:58.260 --> 00:59:59.850
I'm given the
choice of explaining

00:59:59.850 --> 01:00:02.760
a phenomenon in Washington
by either conspiracy

01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:05.584
or incompetence, it is
always incompetence.

01:00:14.540 --> 01:00:17.690
People here don't have the
intelligence or the energy

01:00:17.690 --> 01:00:20.870
to carry out a
successful conspiracy.

01:00:20.870 --> 01:00:24.170
So always assume incompetence.

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:29.600
And second, I think
it's particularly,

01:00:29.600 --> 01:00:34.010
offensive to talk about
conspiracy theories regarding

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:35.210
9/11.

01:00:35.210 --> 01:00:39.320
Usually, that is implying that
there was some collaboration

01:00:39.320 --> 01:00:41.230
by the American
government or agency

01:00:41.230 --> 01:00:42.630
of the American government.

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:46.250
And I think it's a
disgraceful implication that

01:00:46.250 --> 01:00:49.370
deserves no respect
and no investigation.

01:00:49.370 --> 01:00:51.865
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:00.360 --> 01:01:03.760
AUDIENCE: Charles, I hear
you on the Fox News often,

01:01:03.760 --> 01:01:07.810
critiquing some of our
Republican candidates

01:01:07.810 --> 01:01:09.070
for president.

01:01:09.070 --> 01:01:13.650
Would you care to give us a
rather succinct recommendation

01:01:13.650 --> 01:01:18.250
to the various
candidates [INAUDIBLE]??

01:01:18.250 --> 01:01:21.190
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: I
knew that was coming.

01:01:21.190 --> 01:01:22.810
I always try to hide from it.

01:01:22.810 --> 01:01:24.640
I hope maybe, we'll
run out of questions

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:27.790
before we get to it because
I only make enemies.

01:01:27.790 --> 01:01:30.764
You should see my mail
when I say anything

01:01:30.764 --> 01:01:31.930
about any of the candidates.

01:01:31.930 --> 01:01:36.880
Let me just put it in the
most benign way I can.

01:01:36.880 --> 01:01:38.920
I go by the Buckley rule.

01:01:38.920 --> 01:01:41.590
Bill Buckley had this one rule--

01:01:41.590 --> 01:01:44.680
I always vote for the most
conservative candidate

01:01:44.680 --> 01:01:45.350
who can win.

01:01:48.510 --> 01:01:50.470
Keep that in mind--

01:01:50.470 --> 01:01:51.390
who can win.

01:01:51.390 --> 01:01:55.020
Actually, you remember he ran
for the mayor of New York City

01:01:55.020 --> 01:01:58.020
and while he was
running, he was asked,

01:01:58.020 --> 01:02:01.530
what's the first thing he would
do if he were elected mayor?

01:02:01.530 --> 01:02:03.300
And he said, I'd
demand a recount.

01:02:09.100 --> 01:02:12.100
That was just an aside because
we're running out of time,

01:02:12.100 --> 01:02:14.130
I had to get that one in.

01:02:14.130 --> 01:02:17.790
The Buckley rule applies and
it especially applies now.

01:02:17.790 --> 01:02:20.250
If you look for the
ideal candidate,

01:02:20.250 --> 01:02:22.380
I think you are
going the wrong way.

01:02:22.380 --> 01:02:24.930
Obama is the most
left wing president

01:02:24.930 --> 01:02:26.520
we've had in our lifetime.

01:02:26.520 --> 01:02:29.460
His ambitions are
to change America

01:02:29.460 --> 01:02:31.770
in a social
democratic direction.

01:02:31.770 --> 01:02:34.320
He's not a socialist,
he's a Social Democrat.

01:02:34.320 --> 01:02:36.930
There's a big distinction
and I think we

01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:38.820
ought to give him that respect.

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:41.110
Social democracy is
what you see in Europe.

01:02:41.110 --> 01:02:43.230
It's not an awful system.

01:02:43.230 --> 01:02:46.690
It's a benign and a lovely
place, but it's not America.

01:02:46.690 --> 01:02:48.060
It's not what we want.

01:02:48.060 --> 01:02:49.710
It's not in our tradition.

01:02:49.710 --> 01:02:52.110
But because he is
in office and we've

01:02:52.110 --> 01:02:53.940
seen what his ambitions are--

01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:57.360
Obamacare cap and trade,
changing education,

01:02:57.360 --> 01:02:59.640
getting government
control over as much

01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:03.570
as they can under the idea
that Clement Attlee idea

01:03:03.570 --> 01:03:05.580
and this idea that
is rampant in Europe

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:09.030
and has been for 50 years, that
government experts, et cetera,

01:03:09.030 --> 01:03:11.790
have a better way
to direct society

01:03:11.790 --> 01:03:14.550
than the market or
free individuals.

01:03:14.550 --> 01:03:16.810
That is the idea underlying it.

01:03:16.810 --> 01:03:19.170
And it's rather
ironic that that idea

01:03:19.170 --> 01:03:21.282
ought to be the
idea of a governing

01:03:21.282 --> 01:03:22.740
administration in
the United States

01:03:22.740 --> 01:03:25.770
precisely at a time in history
when that idea in Europe

01:03:25.770 --> 01:03:27.510
is collapsing in
front of our eyes.

01:03:32.540 --> 01:03:35.270
If you'd argued it 20
years ago, then we'd

01:03:35.270 --> 01:03:38.960
be arguing, theoretically,
that it doesn't work.

01:03:38.960 --> 01:03:40.880
But the theory is
no longer needed.

01:03:40.880 --> 01:03:41.780
Look at Greece.

01:03:41.780 --> 01:03:43.730
Look at Spain, Portugal.

01:03:43.730 --> 01:03:45.690
Look at France, Germany.

01:03:45.690 --> 01:03:46.940
Everybody is teetering there.

01:03:46.940 --> 01:03:49.340
We know what happens
when you do this, which

01:03:49.340 --> 01:03:51.740
makes it all the more ironic
that it's actually now

01:03:51.740 --> 01:03:54.560
the governing philosophy
of a sitting president.

01:03:54.560 --> 01:03:58.310
And all the more important that
that be stopped next November.

01:04:04.660 --> 01:04:06.640
If Barack Obama
is re-elected, we

01:04:06.640 --> 01:04:09.730
will have Obamacare
and I guarantee you,

01:04:09.730 --> 01:04:14.590
that within a decade because
of its absurd inefficiencies

01:04:14.590 --> 01:04:16.690
and the way it will
wreck the medical system,

01:04:16.690 --> 01:04:20.770
there will be only one way
out after it's instituted

01:04:20.770 --> 01:04:22.700
in a second Obama term.

01:04:22.700 --> 01:04:26.410
The only way out will
be socialized medicine,

01:04:26.410 --> 01:04:28.570
as in Canada, as in Britain.

01:04:28.570 --> 01:04:31.360
So that is a guarantee
if this election is not

01:04:31.360 --> 01:04:34.180
won by the Conservative Party.

01:04:34.180 --> 01:04:35.770
There are other
kinds of consequences

01:04:35.770 --> 01:04:36.790
I don't have to go into.

01:04:36.790 --> 01:04:40.420
You know them-- Supreme Court,
energy policy, et cetera.

01:04:40.420 --> 01:04:44.080
Foreign policy, which I didn't
even mention here tonight.

01:04:44.080 --> 01:04:46.480
All of that is at
stake and I think

01:04:46.480 --> 01:04:49.660
for those who want to
be ideological purists,

01:04:49.660 --> 01:04:53.470
Jim DeMint said when he
supported Christine O'Donnell

01:04:53.470 --> 01:04:56.290
in Delaware and I
said, in a column, that

01:04:56.290 --> 01:04:57.550
was extremely irresponsible.

01:04:57.550 --> 01:04:59.620
She didn't have a
chance of winning

01:04:59.620 --> 01:05:04.300
and we needed every Senate
Republican that we could,

01:05:04.300 --> 01:05:10.120
even if it was a rhino
like governor Newcastle.

01:05:10.120 --> 01:05:12.004
Even though it was a
liberal Republican.

01:05:12.004 --> 01:05:13.420
In the Northeast,
you aren't going

01:05:13.420 --> 01:05:16.270
to get anything other
than a liberal Republican.

01:05:16.270 --> 01:05:21.010
And yet, superior to having
a Democrat because unless you

01:05:21.010 --> 01:05:24.010
have Republicans in, you
get Democratic control

01:05:24.010 --> 01:05:25.570
of the Senate.

01:05:25.570 --> 01:05:27.640
He said, at that
time, I'd rather

01:05:27.640 --> 01:05:32.170
have 30 real Republicans
in the Senate than 60

01:05:32.170 --> 01:05:33.280
who might not be.

01:05:33.280 --> 01:05:37.540
And I would say, that's nice
to say, but at this time,

01:05:37.540 --> 01:05:40.090
when Obama and the
Democrats are changing

01:05:40.090 --> 01:05:42.494
America, that is irresponsible.

01:05:46.850 --> 01:05:49.690
SPEAKER: This will be
our final question.

01:05:49.690 --> 01:05:54.370
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: But that's
not my final answer quite yet.

01:05:54.370 --> 01:05:56.920
Sometimes I pause because
I have no idea where I am

01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:58.910
or where I'm going.

01:05:58.910 --> 01:06:01.610
And that was one of them.

01:06:01.610 --> 01:06:03.130
But in the end,
what's important is

01:06:03.130 --> 01:06:08.574
to win and not to win
with just on principle

01:06:08.574 --> 01:06:10.390
for the power in and of itself.

01:06:10.390 --> 01:06:12.790
It's because it's a
moment in history where

01:06:12.790 --> 01:06:16.750
the ideological trajectory
and the future of the country

01:06:16.750 --> 01:06:18.240
will be decided.

01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:20.590
We often say this
election is the most

01:06:20.590 --> 01:06:25.640
important in our lifetime and
it's often, an exaggeration.

01:06:25.640 --> 01:06:27.550
This time, it's really not.

01:06:27.550 --> 01:06:30.090
And therefore, among
the candidates,

01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:34.540
think about if they
meet your threshold

01:06:34.540 --> 01:06:36.820
as a conservative-- which
is the Buckley rule--

01:06:36.820 --> 01:06:40.730
a conservative who has the
best chance of winning.

01:06:40.730 --> 01:06:43.600
Then once you have
the list of candidates

01:06:43.600 --> 01:06:48.140
who meet your threshold, think
which one has the best chance.

01:06:48.140 --> 01:06:51.250
And I would support
that candidate.

01:06:51.250 --> 01:06:53.620
Now, I don't want to go
through a list of who

01:06:53.620 --> 01:06:55.720
they are, but keep it in mind.

01:06:55.720 --> 01:06:58.790
And I'll simply add
one empirical fact.

01:06:58.790 --> 01:07:01.300
If you look at all
the polls, whenever

01:07:01.300 --> 01:07:04.030
you run a generic
Republican against Obama,

01:07:04.030 --> 01:07:06.100
the generic wins.

01:07:06.100 --> 01:07:08.890
When you run a real
Republican with a real name

01:07:08.890 --> 01:07:12.480
and a real history against
Obama, it's rather uncertain.

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:15.430
Often, they lose in the poll.

01:07:15.430 --> 01:07:17.950
So my advice is to
elect a candidate who's

01:07:17.950 --> 01:07:18.880
the most generic.

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:23.550
Who stands out the least.

01:07:23.550 --> 01:07:25.310
Who has the least baggage.

01:07:25.310 --> 01:07:28.920
Who has the fewest peccadilloes.

01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:32.890
Who presents the
smallest target.

01:07:32.890 --> 01:07:34.570
And I'll give you one example.

01:07:34.570 --> 01:07:38.580
This country has tried
charisma four years ago

01:07:38.580 --> 01:07:40.680
and it doesn't work.

01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:43.230
I would like a candidate
with no charisma.

01:07:46.740 --> 01:07:49.860
My childhood hero
was Henry Jackson--

01:07:49.860 --> 01:07:52.320
democratic senator from
the state of Washington,

01:07:52.320 --> 01:07:54.120
whom I loved.

01:07:54.120 --> 01:07:56.550
And it was said of
Henry Jackson, who

01:07:56.550 --> 01:08:01.680
was exceedingly dull, that if
he ever gave a fireside chat,

01:08:01.680 --> 01:08:02.910
the fire would go out.

01:08:07.660 --> 01:08:10.980
So the very short
answer to your question,

01:08:10.980 --> 01:08:15.600
find the candidate who
makes the fire go out.

01:08:15.600 --> 01:08:17.149
I think we have
one more question.

01:08:21.029 --> 01:08:22.020
AUDIENCE: Doctor--

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:25.560
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER: I
have no idea where you are.

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:28.380
I have no idea I am, actually.

01:08:28.380 --> 01:08:30.490
AUDIENCE: Here's the question.

01:08:30.490 --> 01:08:32.680
If we can only win one--

01:08:32.680 --> 01:08:35.700
either the presidency
or the Senate--

01:08:35.700 --> 01:08:38.040
which one would you prefer?

01:08:38.040 --> 01:08:39.825
CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER:
The presidency.

01:08:39.825 --> 01:08:44.410
The presidency for many reasons.

01:08:44.410 --> 01:08:49.000
First of all, you run foreign
policy almost single handedly.

01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:50.279
You're the commander in chief.

01:08:50.279 --> 01:08:53.040
I think what's going to
happen to the Pentagon budget

01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:58.890
under a second Obama term
will be truly catastrophic.

01:08:58.890 --> 01:09:00.660
And I'm not even
sure it's either or.

01:09:00.660 --> 01:09:03.960
I think we're either going
to get a Republican sweep

01:09:03.960 --> 01:09:07.800
or we're going to get a
Republican Senate and an Obama

01:09:07.800 --> 01:09:08.370
re-elect.

01:09:08.370 --> 01:09:12.010
I think we're looking
at those two outcomes.

01:09:12.010 --> 01:09:14.040
Remember in the
Senate, the Republicans

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:16.140
are defending only 10 seats.

01:09:16.140 --> 01:09:18.554
The Democrats are defending 23.

01:09:18.554 --> 01:09:20.220
And they have a lot
of wobblers and they

01:09:20.220 --> 01:09:25.200
got a lot of incumbent
senators in trouble.

01:09:25.200 --> 01:09:28.100
And a lot of open seats
held by Democrats,

01:09:28.100 --> 01:09:31.200
where the Democrat is either
so old or so in trouble

01:09:31.200 --> 01:09:33.279
that he or she is not running.

01:09:33.279 --> 01:09:36.300
So the chances of winning
simply numerically,

01:09:36.300 --> 01:09:38.620
the Senate are pretty high.

01:09:38.620 --> 01:09:41.340
I think the prize
is the presidency.

01:09:41.340 --> 01:09:43.439
And I think a lot hangs on it.

01:09:43.439 --> 01:09:46.020
And I do think in
some sense, this

01:09:46.020 --> 01:09:48.180
is a chance to
return to my theme

01:09:48.180 --> 01:09:52.800
where a new kind of
constitutional conservatism

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:56.287
can show itself and
can lead the country.

01:09:56.287 --> 01:09:57.120
Thank you very much.

01:09:57.120 --> 01:09:59.870
[APPLAUSE]

